WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.238 --> 00:00:11.330
My name's Conor.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.288
I'm an audiologist,

00:00:12.288 --> 00:00:14.240
and I'm an expert in ears.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.490
And today I'm going to show you

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.370
some earwax-removal videos.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:20.790
There is liquidy, soupy earwax;

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.970
hard, brick-like earwax;

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.960
and a particular fungus
called Aspergillus niger.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.193
Earwax is roughly half skin;

00:00:29.193 --> 00:00:30.310
a little bit of sebum,

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.300
which is a fatty, oily substance;

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.041
and something very special

00:00:34.041 --> 00:00:36.320
called ceruminous secretion.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.345
And ceruminous glands secrete

00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:40.080
this kind of sweaty liquid,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.090
but it's got special molecules in it

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.760
which fight bacteria and
try and keep your ears safe.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.060
You only ever need to remove your earwax

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.100
if it's bothering you.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.130
So if you have hearing loss

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:54.640
or your ear is feeling
full or uncomfortable

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.300
or you feel like there's an
obvious blockage or pressure.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.720
That is abnormal,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.060
and you should go and see your doctor

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.113
or audiologist or practice nurse.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:05.843
So, this is something I would describe

00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:07.660
as sort of the soupy,

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.870
kind of quagmire-type wax.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.353
So you can see actually

00:01:11.353 --> 00:01:14.144
that the debris has a texture to it,

00:01:14.144 --> 00:01:16.960
like a wavy texture and wavy lines in it.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.100
So that means that it's
basically dead skin

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.950
that's emulsified,

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.310
and it's been treated
essentially with fizzy drops.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.380
So sodium bicarb ear drops probably.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.685
And what that does is it
makes the debris liquefy

00:01:28.685 --> 00:01:31.602
and then slide further down the ear canal

00:01:31.602 --> 00:01:32.690
and then against the drum.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:33.989
And that really does cause

00:01:33.989 --> 00:01:35.831
quite a significant hearing loss

00:01:35.831 --> 00:01:37.910
and feeling of discomfort.

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.100
And what's happened here is that

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.890
it's kind of lost a little
bit of its structure,

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.900
which is quite difficult to deal with,

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.417
because usually this type of earwax

00:01:45.417 --> 00:01:47.990
is quite resistant to suction.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:50.460
The machine I'm using is
called a Guardian Projet.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.926
Basically, you have a tank.

00:01:51.926 --> 00:01:53.028
Is that hurting?

00:01:53.028 --> 00:01:55.110
And you have to fill that tank with water,

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:56.593
which should be body temperature,

00:01:56.593 --> 00:01:58.143
about 37 degrees Celsius.

00:01:58.143 --> 00:01:59.950
And if it's too hot or too cold,

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.130
then it will induce a caloric effect,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.760
which is where basically you're heating

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.640
or cooling the fluid
inside your inner ear,

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.630
and that causes unpleasant vertigo,

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:10.646
which is like a spinning dizziness,

00:02:10.646 --> 00:02:11.479
like you're drunk.

00:02:11.479 --> 00:02:13.640
So you put the instrument
in the ear canal,

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.810
and you aim the water at
the roof of the ear canal.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:19.030
So never straight down the ear.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.280
Never aim it at the eardrum.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.310
That's really when accidents happen,

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.380
potentially perforation of the eardrum.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.130
So you aim it at the
roof of the ear canal,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.720
and you sort of move it
like a window washer.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.028
And what that does is it creates

00:02:30.028 --> 00:02:31.680
these vortices in the water

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.430
and a gentle swelling effect.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:34.770
It goes into the ear

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.280
and then just flushes straight
back out into the cup.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.950
So it's on the lowest setting,

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.830
and it's just squirting into the ear.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:43.405
And we leave it there and
let the water do the work.

00:02:43.405 --> 00:02:45.339
Most people find it quite pleasant.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.583
There's a little bit of debris or slime

00:02:51.583 --> 00:02:52.608
left on the eardrum.

00:02:52.608 --> 00:02:55.590
In that situation, we'd
probably go in with suction

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.940
or maybe continue the irrigation
for a little bit longer

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.030
if the patient can tolerate it.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.700
You'll definitely want to have
some earwax left in there.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.850
Certainly not up against the drum

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.410
or anywhere near the eardrum.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.060
So a nice smearing or slick of wax

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.785
around the ear canal
will leave the patient

00:03:11.785 --> 00:03:13.570
protected against the ear drying out,

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:15.950
protected against infection, itching,

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.000
and obviously protection
against foreign bodies as well.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.600
So that's a pretty nice-looking
eardrum right there.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.383
When I first saw it, I thought,

00:03:24.383 --> 00:03:25.900
oh, this will be an easy job,

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:27.750
because the wax is right there.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.350
You can see it with your naked eye,

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.700
so it's all good.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.990
But then as soon as I
tapped on it with the probe,

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:37.460
I thought, right, we're
in for a heavy ride here.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:38.630
The only way I can describe it

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.710
is sort of really like a brick.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.534
It was extremely tough.

00:03:43.534 --> 00:03:45.892
And I started with the suction,

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:48.610
but that was, for all intents
and purposes, pointless.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:50.380
So in this particular case,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.910
I used something called
a Cawthorne hook No. 6.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:56.897
And it's basically a
very, very, very small

00:03:56.897 --> 00:03:59.050
metal right angle, essentially.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:00.960
The end is perhaps a little pointy,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.383
but it's not needle-sharp.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.440
This was extremely impacted.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.510
The reason it's dark,

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:08.976
much darker than the previous case,

00:04:08.976 --> 00:04:10.042
is because it's old.

00:04:10.042 --> 00:04:11.900
The older the wax is, the darker it is,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.623
and that's because it oxidizes.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.930
I try to be very, very gentle.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.590
The Cawthorne hook is small enough

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:22.410
such that you can get
hook around the debris

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.520
and disturb it and leverage it out.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:26.930
So really, to put it bluntly,

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.475
digging it out with a metal instrument

00:04:29.475 --> 00:04:31.380
was the only way, really.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.530
You tend to find with this very hard

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.713
impacted brick-like wax,

00:04:35.713 --> 00:04:37.300
that you can have pain as well.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.091
When the patient tries to self-treat

00:04:39.091 --> 00:04:40.824
with water-based drops,

00:04:40.824 --> 00:04:43.083
the plug will actually
expand a little bit,

00:04:43.083 --> 00:04:44.892
so it'll absorb some of those drops

00:04:44.892 --> 00:04:47.880
and then increase in size temporarily.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.920
And if the plug is very
deep in the ear canal,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.795
that can really, really hurt

00:04:51.795 --> 00:04:53.628
and cause a tremendous ache.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.890
The thing is, you always
have to go in stages,

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:04.520
because if you shove the hook in

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.403
and then pull just in one go,

00:05:06.403 --> 00:05:07.919
you can cause barotrauma,

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:10.210
which is basically damage via pressure,

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:12.440
and that is excruciatingly painful.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.860
So you really have to
go very, very carefully

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:16.360
and tread lightly.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:24.040
What we can see here is
the sort of yellow frills

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.440
that are coming off the main plug.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.820
That's just dead skin.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.720
When we see an impacted plug like this,

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.280
there's usually sheets and sheets

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:33.779
of dead skin enveloping it,

00:05:33.779 --> 00:05:36.580
sort of like lasagna, basically.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.971
And then the darker brown bit,

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:41.687
that's very, very dense,

00:05:41.687 --> 00:05:43.670
dry hard earwax,

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:46.745
which is just formed over
a long period of time.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:07.010
It's impossible to say how old it is,

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:11.304
but, yeah, it's definitely
been in there a while.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.200
The scientific name for it,

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.120
the particular fungus is
called Aspergillus niger.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.810
The casual name for it is black mold.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.890
Aspergillus niger lives everywhere.

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.270
It wouldn't a be shock for me

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:28.050
if there was some on your hand right now

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.680
or on your desk. It's very common.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:31.960
So the likelihood is it was
either in the ear already

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:34.100
or it was on a fingernail
or under a fingernail

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.510
and the patient scratched their ear.

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:40.633
The reason it's got out of
control is anyone's guess.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:43.930
So for this I'm using a suction probe

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:45.890
to get the debris out of the ear.

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:47.000
And really what I wanted to do

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:51.040
is remove the bed of the
fungus, the mycelium.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.310
And that's why you'll see
this white clothy material

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:55.920
with the sort of goo underneath it.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.180
That's the very thick, well-developed

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:01.000
rooty part of the fungus on the eardrum,

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:02.640
which is risky to some degree

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:03.980
because that's the kind of situation

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:06.500
where you can see a perforation.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:08.633
And usually what we tend to find

00:07:08.633 --> 00:07:10.191
is that patients who have these

00:07:10.191 --> 00:07:13.190
very well-developed fungal infections

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:14.840
is more often than not

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.723
because they've used a lot
of antibiotic eardrops.

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:21.160
The reason why it's bad
to use antibiotic eardrops

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:22.250
when you don't need it

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.903
is because you are disrupting
the natural balance

00:07:24.903 --> 00:07:27.450
of microorganisms in your ear.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.360
And if you kill off all the good bacteria,

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:31.460
then the fungus is going,
"Hey, this is a party,"

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:33.623
and grow unchallenged.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.770
I couldn't get all of the mycelium out

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:38.780
because it was sort of around the bend

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:40.870
and it was in a very,
very awkward position.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:44.270
But I achieved most of what I wanted to,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.630
which was to expose a
nice healthy eardrum.

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:48.140
And then of course that paved the way

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:50.873
for the antifungal drops to do their job.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:53.670
This advice cannot be said enough.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:55.620
If your ear isn't bothering you,

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.110
don't poke it, don't prod it,

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:59.093
don't treat it with anything,

00:07:59.093 --> 00:08:00.676
don't put anything in it,

00:08:00.676 --> 00:08:01.760
and just leave it be.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:05.325
Most people will maybe
have an earwax blockage

00:08:05.325 --> 00:08:07.550
maybe once or twice or
three times in their lives,

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:08.750
but it's not something

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:10.770
that you need to keep on top of regularly,

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.330
because your ears are self-cleaning

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.883
and they will usually
take care of themselves.

